Docker Volume with Code snippets and real life use case

Docker container are non-persistent by default in nature

non-persistent — If data is stored inside container and the container is removed or deleted then all the data and configuration will be missed too.


Photo by Rubaitul Azad on Unsplash
Docker Volumes
Volumes provide a way to persist data generated by and used by Docker containers.
One of the benefits of Docker Volumes is that they can be used to store data separately from the container itself. This means that even if a container is deleted, the data stored in the volume will remain.
Docker Volume Types
In Docker, there are mainly two types of volumes that can be used to manage data persistency between containers and the host machine:

Named Volumes: Named volumes are explicitly created and given a user-defined name. They are managed by Docker, but users can specify the name when creating the volume. Named volumes offer easy data management and can be shared across multiple containers. They are the recommended approach for most use cases.
Host-Bound Volumes: Host-bound volumes allow you to directly mount a directory from the host machine into the container. This provides easy access to host files from within the container, but it may create dependencies on the host environment.
